{"date": "2026-11-12", "post_id": 1510156, "title": "Moon, Venus and Spica Meet", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-12-moon-venus-and-spica-meet/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://tolkiengateway.net/wiki/Riddle_of_Strider\">Not all those who wander are lost.</a> A night sky example is that <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/planets/\">planets</a> wander, and in fact the very word \"planet\" comes from \"wandering star\" in Greek. As planets <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-may-30-mars-in-the-loop/\">shift</a> relative to <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/planetary-motion/\">fixed stars</a>, they may appear from our perspective to <a href=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1504595403659-9088ce801e29\">get closer</a> to other astronomical objects. Such <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-march-4-10-days-of-venus-and-jupiter/\">meetings</a> are called <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2020-december-15-great-conjunction-saturn-and-jupiter-converge/\">conjunctions</a>. They don't happen by chance, but follow the precise laws of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/orbits-and-keplers-laws/\">celestial mechanics</a>. This peaceful <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DfciOD3DKOE/?img_index=1\">image</a>, captured in  <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poland\">Poland</a>, shows the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-april-24-young-moon-and-sister-stars/\">crescent Moon</a> above the <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x1YiJgA8lpw\">Tatra Mountains</a>, and bright <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/venus/\">Venus</a> close by, a little lower in the horizon. The bright star to the left of Venus is <a href=\"https://www.cfa.harvard.edu/news/understanding-spica\">Spica</a> in the constellation of <a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/education/constellations/virgo/\">Virgo</a>, the Maiden. Spica is <a href=\"https://earthsky.org/brightest-stars/speed-on-to-spica-the-15th-brightest-star/\">one of the brightest stars</a> in the sky, only about 250 light-years away. It was found in 1890 that <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spica\">Spica</a> is a binary star: two massive blue stars orbiting each other every 4 days. But earlier this year it was reported that Spica, the current companion of wandering Venus, is actually a <a href=\"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2515-5172/aea83b\">triple system</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> what's next?", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/trzeci_plan/\">W\u0142odzimierz Bubak</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/trzeci_plan/\">W\u0142odzimierz Bubak</a>", "alt": "A crescent moon and bright Venus appear beyond a mountain in a rural area at dusk.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-12-moon-venus-and-spica-meet/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/conjunction-bubak.jpg?w=1638&h=2048&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 November 12 - Moon, Venus and Spica Meet</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-12-moon-venus-and-spica-meet/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Cecilia Chirenti\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Not all those who wander are lost. A night sky example is that planets wander, and in fact the very word &quot;planet&quot; comes from &quot;wandering star&quot; in Greek. As planets shift relative to fixed stars, they may appear from our perspective to get closer to other astronomical objects. Such meetings are called conjunctions. They don&#039;t happen by chance, but follow the precise laws of celestial mechanics. This peaceful image, captured in  Poland, shows the crescent Moon above the Tatra Mountains, and bright Venus close by, a little lower in the horizon. The bright star to the left of Venus is Spica in the constellation of Virgo, the Maiden. Spica is one of the brightest stars in the sky, only about 250 light-years away. It was found in 1890 that Spica is a binary star: two massive blue stars orbiting each other every 4 days. But earlier this year it was reported that Spica, the current companion of wandering Venus, is actually a triple system.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: what&#039;s next?\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"moon, venus and spica meet\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Moon, Venus and Spica Meet\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-12-moon-venus-and-spica-meet/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/conjunction-bubak.jpg?w=1638&#038;h=2048&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/conjunction-bubak.jpg?w=1638&#038;h=2048&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"A crescent moon and bright Venus appear beyond a mountain in a rural area at dusk.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-11-12T00:05:00-05:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-11-11T15:15:16-05:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Moon, Venus and Spica Meet\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/conjunction-bubak.jpg?w=1638&#038;h=2048&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 November 12<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/conjunction-bubak.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/conjunction-bubak.jpg\" alt=\"A crescent moon and bright Venus appear beyond a mountain in a rural area at dusk.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Moon, Venus and Spica Meet </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/trzeci_plan/\">W\u0142odzimierz Bubak</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://tolkiengateway.net/wiki/Riddle_of_Strider\">Not all those who wander are lost.</a> A night sky example is that <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/planets/\">planets</a> wander, and in fact the very word \"planet\" comes from \"wandering star\" in Greek. As planets <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-may-30-mars-in-the-loop/\">shift</a> relative to <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/planetary-motion/\">fixed stars</a>, they may appear from our perspective to <a href=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1504595403659-9088ce801e29\">get closer</a> to other astronomical objects. Such <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-march-4-10-days-of-venus-and-jupiter/\">meetings</a> are called <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2020-december-15-great-conjunction-saturn-and-jupiter-converge/\">conjunctions</a>. They don't happen by chance, but follow the precise laws of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/orbits-and-keplers-laws/\">celestial mechanics</a>. This peaceful <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DfciOD3DKOE/?img_index=1\">image</a>, captured in  <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poland\">Poland</a>, shows the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-april-24-young-moon-and-sister-stars/\">crescent Moon</a> above the <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x1YiJgA8lpw\">Tatra Mountains</a>, and bright <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/venus/\">Venus</a> close by, a little lower in the horizon. The bright star to the left of Venus is <a href=\"https://www.cfa.harvard.edu/news/understanding-spica\">Spica</a> in the constellation of <a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/education/constellations/virgo/\">Virgo</a>, the Maiden. Spica is <a href=\"https://earthsky.org/brightest-stars/speed-on-to-spica-the-15th-brightest-star/\">one of the brightest stars</a> in the sky, only about 250 light-years away. It was found in 1890 that <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spica\">Spica</a> is a binary star: two massive blue stars orbiting each other every 4 days. But earlier this year it was reported that Spica, the current companion of wandering Venus, is actually a <a href=\"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2515-5172/aea83b\">triple system</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> what's next?\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-11-ngc-4449-small-galaxy-big-impact/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nCecilia Chirenti, Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261112/html"}